17. There is one conceptual distinction I think the paper needs explicitly

Right now there is a danger of conflating:

$$ \boxed{\text{representation}} $$

with

$$ \boxed{\text{semantics}}. $$

Putting two concepts on a sphere does not make their geometric relationship meaningful.

For example, you can assign:

$$ \mathrm{man}\mapsto(10^\circ,40^\circ) $$

and

$$ \mathrm{king}\mapsto(20^\circ,50^\circ). $$

The angle between them becomes a number.

But why should that angle represent the man→king relationship?

That relationship has to come from:

a stipulated encoding,
learned data,
an optimization objective,
or some other construction.

The paper understands this to some extent—the lexicon/embedding map is explicitly retained—but the philosophical argument should make this distinction crystal clear:

The geometry provides a language for relationships; it does not automatically provide the relationships.

That sentence, conceptually, would protect the framework from many overclaims.

