This is worth emphasizing.

If I strip away the geometry, phase, rotations, colors, language examples, etc., I think the underlying philosophical proposition is approximately:

Different kinds of information require different notions of combination, and an operation should be understood in terms of what informational property it preserves.

That is a serious idea.

The document gives several different examples:

$$ 1+1=2 $$

versus:

$$ \text{red}+\text{green}=\text{yellow} $$

versus:

$$ A\oplus A=A $$

versus:

$$ T_a(T_b(x))\neq T_b(T_a(x)). $$

These aren't merely alternative arithmetic tricks.

They correspond to different informational questions:

$$ \begin{array}{c|c} \text{operation} & \text{question}\\ \hline + & \text{How many?}\\ \oplus & \text{What is present?}\\ \mathrm{Mix} & \text{What composite state results?}\\ T & \text{What happens, and in what order?} \end{array} $$

The document is increasingly making that distinction explicit.

That is the part I would protect most carefully.